[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Vector Mathematics & Transformations in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vector mathematics forms the foundational backbone of game development in Unity. Whether you are moving a player, calculating a line of sight, or rotating a spaceship, you are utilizing vectors. This tutorial covers the essential vector operations and transformations you need to build robust gameplay systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Vector3 Fundamentals"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Unity, 3D space is represented using the Vector3 struct, which holds three float values: x, y, and z. A vector can represent two distinct concepts: a position in space, or a direction with a magnitude (length)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Vector3 position = new Vector3(0.0f, 5.0f, -10.0f);
Vector3 direction = new Vector3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
float magnitude = direction.magnitude;
Vector3 normalizedDir = direction.normalized;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Dot Product and Angle Calculations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The dot product takes two vectors and returns a single float value. This value tells you about the relative direction of the two vectors. If the dot product is positive, they point in generally the same direction. If it is zero, they are perpendicular. If it is negative, they point in opposite directions."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Transform playerTransform = transform;
Transform enemyTransform = enemy;
Vector3 toEnemy = (enemyTransform.position - playerTransform.position).normalized;
float dotResult = Vector3.Dot(playerTransform.forward, toEnemy);

if (dotResult > 0.5f) {
    Debug.Log("Enemy is roughly in front of the player!");
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Cross Product for Orientation"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The cross product takes two vectors and returns a third vector that is orthogonal (perpendicular) to both. In Unity, this is exceptionally useful for finding a surface normal or determining which side of an object another object is on."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Vector3 forward = transform.forward;
Vector3 targetDir = (target.position - transform.position).normalized;
Vector3 crossResult = Vector3.Cross(forward, targetDir);

if (crossResult.y > 0) {
    Debug.Log("Target is to the right");
} else {
    Debug.Log("Target is to the left");
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Matrix Transformations and Space Conversions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Game objects exist in hierarchies. Understanding how to convert coordinates between local space (relative to a parent object) and world space (global coordinates) is critical for spawning projectiles, applying forces, or handling relative movement."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transform.TransformPoint: Converts a position from local space to world space."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transform.InverseTransformDirection: Converts a directional vector from world space to local space."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"Vector3 localTargetPos = new Vector3(0f, 0f, 5f);
Vector3 worldTargetPos = transform.TransformPoint(localTargetPos);
Debug.DrawLine(transform.position, worldTargetPos, Color.red);"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering vectors unlocks advanced gameplay programming capabilities in Unity. Practice combining dot products for sightlines, cross products for steering behaviors, and space conversions to build responsive, accurate game mechanics."}]
